1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,402
Behind a reverse proxy or a load balancer, your site sees the proxy

2
00:00:05,402 --> 00:00:07,151
instead of the visitor.

3
00:00:07,151 --> 00:00:12,758
The Reverse Proxy Configuration feature tells the site which forwarded headers to read.

4
00:00:12,758 --> 00:00:17,100
In this video, we enable it, choose the headers, and keep it safe.

5
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:25,927
A reverse proxy or a load balancer sits in front of your site.

6
00:00:25,927 --> 00:00:30,527
Visitors connect to the proxy, and the proxy passes each request on.

7
00:00:30,527 --> 00:00:35,500
So the site sees the address of the proxy, not the address of the visitor.

8
00:00:36,250 --> 00:00:40,849
The proxy can pass the original details along in HTTP headers.

9
00:00:40,849 --> 00:00:48,086
X-Forwarded-For carries the client IP address, X-Forwarded-Proto carries the protocol, HTTP or HTTPS,

10
00:00:48,086 --> 00:00:52,550
and X-Forwarded-Host carries the host the visitor asked for.

11
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:56,267
Some features depend on the visitor's address.

12
00:00:56,267 --> 00:01:00,660
The Audit Trail can record the client IP address, and Rate Limits count

13
00:01:00,660 --> 00:01:03,684
the requests of anonymous visitors per address.

14
00:01:03,684 --> 00:01:08,590
Behind a proxy, without the headers, all of them share the address of the proxy.

15
00:01:12,799 --> 00:01:16,226
The settings come with a feature that you enable first.

16
00:01:16,226 --> 00:01:19,709
Let's open Tools, then Features, and search for reverse.

17
00:01:20,180 --> 00:01:24,650
Reverse Proxy Configuration is listed in the Infrastructure category.

18
00:01:24,650 --> 00:01:26,200
Let's select Enable.

19
00:01:26,666 --> 00:01:30,947
A message confirms that the feature was enabled, and the Settings menu has

20
00:01:30,947 --> 00:01:32,445
a Reverse Proxy entry.

21
00:01:32,445 --> 00:01:37,581
Opening the page takes two permissions, Manage Reverse Proxy Settings and Manage settings,

22
00:01:37,581 --> 00:01:40,416
and the Administrator role has both by default.

23
00:01:44,637 --> 00:01:47,215
Let's open Settings, then Reverse Proxy.

24
00:01:47,215 --> 00:01:49,008
A warning at the top says:

25
00:01:49,008 --> 00:01:54,333
The website might be restarted upon saving the settings, potentially leading to temporary

26
00:01:54,333 --> 00:01:56,687
unresponsiveness during the process.

27
00:01:57,140 --> 00:01:59,981
The page has one option for each header:

28
00:01:59,981 --> 00:02:03,872
X-Forwarded-For, X-Forwarded-Proto, and X-Forwarded-Host.

29
00:02:03,872 --> 00:02:09,677
Each label reads Enables the forwarding of the HTTP header, followed by the header name.

30
00:02:09,677 --> 00:02:11,160
All three are off.

31
00:02:11,626 --> 00:02:16,598
Under each option, Current request value shows what the site sees for this page:

32
00:02:16,598 --> 00:02:19,836
the client IP address, the protocol, and the host.

33
00:02:19,836 --> 00:02:24,346
Here there is no proxy, so these are the values of the local connection.

34
00:02:24,805 --> 00:02:26,856
Let's turn on all three options.

35
00:02:26,856 --> 00:02:30,904
Behind a proxy that sends the headers, turning an option on makes the

36
00:02:30,904 --> 00:02:33,225
site use the forwarded value instead.

37
00:02:33,681 --> 00:02:34,707
Select Save.

38
00:02:34,707 --> 00:02:36,075
The site confirms:

39
00:02:36,075 --> 00:02:38,412
Site settings updated successfully.

40
00:02:38,412 --> 00:02:42,631
The three options stay selected, and they are saved for this tenant.

41
00:02:46,831 --> 00:02:51,042
Forwarded headers are only as trustworthy as whoever sends them.

42
00:02:51,042 --> 00:02:57,299
The documentation warns that wrong settings can expose a site to IP spoofing and header injection.

43
00:02:57,299 --> 00:03:01,451
So trust the headers only when they come from your own proxies.

44
00:03:02,201 --> 00:03:06,272
Known proxies lists the IP addresses of the proxies you trust, and known

45
00:03:06,272 --> 00:03:08,464
networks lists their network ranges.

46
00:03:08,464 --> 00:03:10,500
This page has no fields for them.

47
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:14,623
Set them in the Settings recipe step, or in the server configuration when

48
00:03:14,623 --> 00:03:16,241
the application reads it.

49
00:03:16,991 --> 00:03:20,924
When the application is set up to read these settings from the server

50
00:03:20,924 --> 00:03:23,441
configuration, its values take precedence.

51
00:03:23,441 --> 00:03:25,382
This page then shows a message:

52
00:03:25,382 --> 00:03:30,468
The current settings are coming from configuration sources, saving the settings will affect

53
00:03:30,468 --> 00:03:32,881
the AdminSettings not the configuration.

54
00:03:33,631 --> 00:03:37,718
The options of this page belong to the current tenant, so each tenant

55
00:03:37,718 --> 00:03:39,735
of an installation has its own.

56
00:03:39,735 --> 00:03:43,931
A deployment plan can export them with the Reverse Proxy settings step.

57
00:03:45,931 --> 00:03:50,997
Enable Reverse Proxy Configuration, turn on the headers your proxy sends, and trust

58
00:03:50,997 --> 00:03:52,591
only your own proxies.

59
00:03:52,591 --> 00:03:54,071
Thanks for watching.

